>>
endobj
196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (An Isolation Forest, an anomaly detection technique was selected because healthy recordings provide a reference distribution, while confirmed fault examples are confined to one short episode. Fitting a supervised fault classifier to that episode could learn characteristics specific to its operating conditions. Isolation Forest instead learns the healthy feature distribution and assigns higher anomaly scores to unusual combinations of features. Its tree-based splits do not require feature scaling.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 71 0 R
/T (3.3 Training procedure)
/E (3.3 Training procedure)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (Records from 29 April through 9 June 2025 were reserved as holdout data. Development recordings were grouped into the supplied January fault, healthy and suspicious periods. Only recordings with finite calculated Z short-wave high-frequency RMS greater than 13 were selected as likely running observations. Features were joined using original row identifiers and timestamps; missing or non-finite required features were excluded rather than imputed.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (For a controlled feature comparison, recordings had to be complete across the union of the three configurations' inputs. This retained 428 of 430 healthy recordings, all 39 fault-labelled recordings, and 271 of 276 suspicious recordings. Operating runs were inferred from calculated RMS at least 13, with a maximum gap of four hours. Healthy runs were ordered chronologically, and the latest approximately 25% were reserved for threshold calibration. Keeping entire runs together reduces leakage between neighboring recordings within a run, although the inferred run boundaries remain an approximation.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
