230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (The proposed architecture uses an event-driven near-real-time inference strategy. Once the data is received through AWS IoT Core, it undergoes signal processing, feature extraction, and validation before being forwarded to the Isolation Forest model hosted on Amazon EC2.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (The model generates an anomaly score as soon as a complete, temporally aligned feature vector becomes available. Since the selected high-frequency vibration channel is uploaded every four hours, predictions are expected approximately every four hours, subject to the availability of the other required sensor channels.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (Near-real-time inference is selected over continuous real-time processing because the sensor data is acquired and uploaded periodically rather than streamed continuously. Batch inference is not preferred because it would introduce additional delays between receiving sensor data and identifying abnormal vibration patterns.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
