/P 74 0 R
/E (The architecture separates sensor acquisition, ingestion, inference, storage, and alerting to support independent scaling.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (At the initial deployment of 50 machines, lightweight feature records produce relatively low cloud inference demand. The Isolation Forest is computationally inexpensive because predictions operate on four numerical features rather than full vibration waveforms.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (For 500+ machines, edge processing capacity can be expanded by adding industrial gateways, while AWS IoT Core handles additional connected devices. Multiple inference workers can be deployed behind a load balancer, with an SQS queue introduced between ingestion and inference to buffer bursts and support reliable processing.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (Amazon S3 provides scalable storage for historical recordings and model artifacts, while a time-series database supports fleet-level monitoring. Gateway buffering and retry mechanisms protect against temporary network disruptions. Machine identifiers, timestamps, event IDs, and model versions support traceability and duplicate detection.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (This architecture balances bandwidth efficiency, operational reliability, centralized model management, and scalability. Its modular design allows additional sensor channels, machine types, and improved models to be introduced without replacing the overall system.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
